WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.980
Right now, I mean, if you look at the tech world, we are pouring billions of dollars into this frantic, global, almost desperate race to build machines that can think.

00:11.120 --> 00:12.900
Yeah, the stale of it is just massive.

00:13.140 --> 00:21.380
It really is. Governments, megacorporations, venture capitalists, they're all chasing this dream of artificial general intelligence.

00:21.560 --> 00:26.080
We want silicon that can reason, adapt, and, you know, actually learn.

00:26.200 --> 00:26.420
Right.

00:26.420 --> 00:34.220
But then on the completely opposite side of the academic campus, there is this entirely different group of people, neurobiologists.

00:34.800 --> 00:46.800
And they've spent their entire lives pulling apart the, well, the wet, squishy reality of biological brains to figure out how nature actually solved this exact problem millions of years ago.

00:47.060 --> 00:48.800
Which is kind of funny when you think about it.

00:48.800 --> 00:56.960
Right. You would assume these two groups are in constant communication, like sharing cafeteria tables, swapping data, building some unified theory of intelligence.

00:56.960 --> 01:00.880
I mean, it seems like the most necessary intellectual collision of our time.

01:00.960 --> 01:06.340
If you were trying to build a brain, you should probably be talking to the people who study the only working models in existence.

01:06.540 --> 01:07.660
Exactly. But they don't talk.

01:07.860 --> 01:08.740
No, not really.

01:08.900 --> 01:16.580
The people building artificial minds and the people studying biological minds inhabit these completely isolated universes.

01:16.580 --> 01:22.100
They publish in different journals that use fundamentally incompatible jargon.

01:22.100 --> 01:24.280
And this is the wild part.

01:24.920 --> 01:30.520
Their core philosophies about what it actually takes to make something intelligent are wildly opposed.

01:30.860 --> 01:34.520
It is so much more profound than just an academic siloing effect.

01:34.700 --> 01:36.960
They aren't just avoiding each other because they're busy.

01:36.960 --> 01:48.760
They are separated by a foundational disagreement over what information actually is, how it's stored, and how a network of any kind, biological or digital, actually becomes functional.

01:49.060 --> 01:53.200
And that philosophical collision is exactly the territory we are mapping out today.

01:53.460 --> 02:02.300
Our source material for this deep dive is Peter Robin Heisinger's incredibly provocative book, The Self-Assembling Brain, How Neural Networks Grow Smarter.

02:02.300 --> 02:12.280
It is a text that really forces a reckoning, because Heisinger uses neurobiology not just to explain the brain, but to hold up a mirror to the current state of artificial intelligence.

02:12.440 --> 02:15.720
Yeah, exposing assumptions that most computer scientists don't even realize they are making.

02:15.980 --> 02:21.140
So the mission for our deep dive today is to dissect what Heisinger calls the information problem.

02:21.800 --> 02:31.640
We need to figure out how a string of genetic code-like, the DNA coiled inside a microscopic cell, actually goes about building a sentient brain.

02:32.300 --> 02:41.900
And more critically, for you, the listener, we are going to explore why modern AI might be taking a massive, perhaps fatal, shortcut in its architecture.

02:42.020 --> 02:46.960
Right, and what fundamental elements of intelligence are just being permanently lost by taking that shortcut.

02:46.960 --> 02:52.300
To ground this, I want you to hold two starkly contrasting images in your mind as we go.

02:52.400 --> 02:58.840
On one side, visualize the biological reality, the chaotic, wet, microscopic biological neuron.

02:59.080 --> 03:00.380
Based in chemical soup.

03:00.380 --> 03:05.740
Exactly, bathed in a chemical soup, taking physical shape from the instructions hidden in the double helix of DNA.

03:06.160 --> 03:08.800
It is a system defined entirely by physical growth.

03:09.040 --> 03:11.720
And then on the other side, picture the realm of modern AI.

03:11.940 --> 03:17.900
It's a perfectly sterile, rigid, mathematical map of an artificial neural network glowing on a server farm somewhere.

03:17.980 --> 03:19.860
It's clean, it's engineered from the top down.

03:20.200 --> 03:23.720
And it is entirely static in its physical structure.

03:23.720 --> 03:32.280
So the multi-billion dollar question is whether we can ever build a bridge between the wet, chaotic biology and the clean, engineered math.

03:32.380 --> 03:39.080
But, you know, to even survey the land where that bridge might go, we first have to completely demolish our everyday understanding of information.

03:39.340 --> 03:44.980
Which brings us to this incredibly illuminating thought experiment introduced right in the prologue of our source text.

03:44.980 --> 03:45.900
I love this one.

03:46.040 --> 03:48.600
It completely rewired how I think about data.

03:49.120 --> 03:52.880
So, picture the smartest alien entity in the universe.

03:53.700 --> 04:01.320
This being possesses technology and analytical capabilities that make our best supercomputers look like, I don't know, abacuses.

04:01.520 --> 04:03.220
Right, basically godlike tech.

04:03.320 --> 04:03.480
Yeah.

04:03.480 --> 04:09.660
One day, this alien touches down on Earth, steps out of its ship, and finds a single apple seed resting in the dirt.

04:10.120 --> 04:14.660
The alien picks it up, totally baffled, and wonders, well, what does this object do?

04:14.820 --> 04:21.140
And armed with absolute technological supremacy, the alien doesn't just guess, it deploys the ultimate scanning machine.

04:21.500 --> 04:24.060
It dissects the seed down to the subatomic level.

04:24.500 --> 04:27.680
It maps every single carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen atom.

04:28.160 --> 04:31.880
It sequences the entire genetic code with zero errors.

04:31.880 --> 04:36.100
So, the alien has a perfect, lossless dataset of the seed.

04:36.160 --> 04:36.600
Exactly.

04:36.800 --> 04:42.300
It feeds all of this molecular and genetic information into an unimaginably powerful quantum computer.

04:42.820 --> 04:48.000
The computer analyzes every statistical distribution, every repeating sequence in the DNA.

04:48.480 --> 04:51.160
It completely masters the mathematics of the object.

04:51.300 --> 04:52.800
But here is the brutal punchline.

04:52.880 --> 04:53.480
Yeah, tell him.

04:53.640 --> 04:59.540
After all of that flawless analysis, the alien still has absolutely no idea what the seed does.

04:59.740 --> 05:00.260
Wait, really?

05:00.640 --> 05:01.120
None at all?

05:01.120 --> 05:01.580
None.

05:01.580 --> 05:05.380
It cannot look at the perfectly decoded DNA and deduce an apple tree.

05:05.700 --> 05:08.280
That paradox is the crux of the information problem.

05:09.160 --> 05:14.600
The alien has total unfettered access to the code, but the code does not describe an apple tree.

05:14.740 --> 05:17.800
Let's linger on this because it kind of assaults our intuition, right?

05:17.860 --> 05:18.520
It really does.

05:18.660 --> 05:21.220
We are taught from grade school that DNA is a blueprint.

05:21.600 --> 05:23.320
Yeah, the classic blueprint analogy.

05:23.740 --> 05:23.960
Right.

05:23.960 --> 05:32.860
If I hire an architect and they hand me a blueprint of a house, I can look at the paper and immediately say, oh, the kitchen is in the back, the bedroom is upstairs, the roof is pitched.

05:33.360 --> 05:35.580
The endpoint is explicitly drawn out.

05:35.700 --> 05:36.820
You can see the whole thing at once.

05:36.900 --> 05:37.220
Exactly.

05:37.220 --> 05:41.720
So if DNA is a blueprint, why doesn't knowing the code reveal the tree?

05:41.920 --> 05:47.220
Because the biological reality is that genetic code is not a blueprint.

05:47.640 --> 05:49.780
It's a recipe for an algorithm.

05:49.780 --> 05:51.360
A recipe for an algorithm.

05:51.520 --> 05:52.400
Okay, break that down.

05:52.780 --> 05:57.100
So this distinction introduces the concept of unfolding information.

05:58.080 --> 06:02.800
When you look at a blueprint, the endpoint information is physically present, just scaled down.

06:03.300 --> 06:07.040
But consider a recipe for like a complex pastry.

06:07.260 --> 06:11.940
A list of ingredients, flour, sugar, butter, yeast, and a set of temporal instructions.

06:12.580 --> 06:14.400
Knead, rest, bake.

06:14.560 --> 06:14.760
Right.

06:14.900 --> 06:17.320
That written recipe looks absolutely nothing like a croissant.

06:17.560 --> 06:18.000
Oh, I see.

06:18.000 --> 06:25.240
The genetic code inside that apple seed isn't a tiny, highly compressed photograph of an apple tree waiting to be unzipped like a JPEG.

06:25.380 --> 06:25.600
Right.

06:25.840 --> 06:28.760
It is a set of starting conditions and conditional rules.

06:29.340 --> 06:36.860
And those rules require time, energy, and a continuous physical interaction with the environment to actually reveal their endpoint.

06:37.300 --> 06:42.100
So the alien has the recipe card, but it fundamentally hasn't turned on the oven.

06:42.300 --> 06:44.140
That is the mechanical reality.

06:44.140 --> 06:48.260
The information to generate the tree is present, but it exists in a dormant state.

06:48.420 --> 06:52.740
It can only unfold through the physical, sequential process of development.

06:52.740 --> 06:54.400
So the alien is just stuck.

06:54.660 --> 06:54.840
Yeah.

06:54.980 --> 07:01.200
Despite all its computational power, it eventually hits a mathematical wall and realizes there is no analytical shortcut.

07:01.740 --> 07:05.260
To find out what this code becomes, I have to let it run in the physical world.

07:05.360 --> 07:05.660
Wow.

07:05.920 --> 07:12.060
So the alien plants the seed in the soil, provides water and sunlight, and watches it grow over years.

07:12.060 --> 07:20.540
Only when branches form, leaves unfurl, and it finally bites into a piece of fruit, does the alien know what the genetic code actually means.

07:20.740 --> 07:26.100
While I was processing this, I kept trying to find a modern equivalent that doesn't involve, you know, alien botany.

07:26.260 --> 07:28.780
And I realized video games are the perfect parallel.

07:28.920 --> 07:29.800
Oh, that's a good one.

07:29.840 --> 07:30.280
Let's hear it.

07:30.280 --> 07:37.240
Imagine you have a friend from an isolated community who has never seen a television or a video game in their life.

07:38.160 --> 07:43.420
You hand them a flash drive containing the exact binary code of Super Mario Bros.

07:43.580 --> 07:43.880
Okay.

07:44.340 --> 07:47.400
Every single one and zero that makes up the game.

07:47.520 --> 07:51.520
So they possess a perfect, lossless copy of the information.

07:51.780 --> 07:51.960
Right.

07:52.080 --> 07:52.860
Just like the alien.

07:52.860 --> 07:54.320
And let's say your friend is a savant.

07:54.960 --> 07:55.940
They read the binary.

07:56.260 --> 07:59.000
They memorize the sequence of millions of ones and zeros.

07:59.380 --> 08:02.300
They can recite the statistical distribution of the code.

08:02.660 --> 08:03.760
But do they know what the game is?

08:04.480 --> 08:09.360
Do they have any concept of what it feels like to make a pixelated plumber jump over a walking mushroom?

08:09.640 --> 08:09.860
No.

08:10.420 --> 08:11.700
You cannot experience the game.

08:11.780 --> 08:15.500
You cannot know what the game is just by analyzing the code statically.

08:15.740 --> 08:17.280
Because the code has to be executed.

08:17.620 --> 08:18.040
Exactly.

08:18.040 --> 08:26.700
The only physical way to extract the true information of the video game is to insert it into a console, supply it with electricity, and run the program over time.

08:27.120 --> 08:31.220
The ones and zeros are meaningless instructions until they are executed in sequence.

08:31.740 --> 08:33.060
You literally have to play it.

08:33.400 --> 08:40.380
That analogy cuts right to the heart of the issue because it highlights the absolute indispensability of execution.

08:40.960 --> 08:47.240
The binary code, much like the DNA, only has meaning when it is pushed through the dimension of time.

08:47.240 --> 08:47.680
Right.

08:48.040 --> 08:53.900
Now, taking that a step further and applying it to the human brain, the philosophical implications are just staggering.

08:54.160 --> 08:54.500
How so?

08:54.820 --> 09:01.820
If there is fundamentally no mathematical way to read a genetic code other than running it, meaning physically growing the organism,

09:02.200 --> 09:09.120
then science is principally unable to predict the exact wiring of an adult brain using analytical computation.

09:09.120 --> 09:09.680
Wait, wait.

09:09.680 --> 09:14.940
If I am hearing you correctly, you are saying that no matter how advanced our supercomputers get,

09:15.480 --> 09:19.880
even if we build Dyson spheres and harness the energy of the sun to power our computers,

09:20.340 --> 09:27.520
we can never look at a strand of human DNA and predict the precise, microscopic wiring of the brain it will eventually produce.

09:27.520 --> 09:30.120
That is the uncomfortable mathematical truth.

09:30.120 --> 09:36.980
The end point, the adult brain with its trillions of synaptic connections, literally does not exist within the genetic code.

09:37.160 --> 09:37.900
It's just not there.

09:38.160 --> 09:38.640
Exactly.

09:39.140 --> 09:43.160
The code only contains the algorithmic rules for the process of assembling the brain.

09:43.160 --> 09:52.120
You can simulate those rules, certainly, but to know the exact final physical state, you have to run the simulation sequentially from start to finish.

09:52.280 --> 09:53.500
You can't just skip to the end.

09:53.720 --> 09:58.520
You cannot simply solve an overarching equation and skip to the final answer.

09:59.040 --> 10:02.140
In biology, the process itself is the information.

10:02.140 --> 10:03.300
Wow.

10:03.780 --> 10:13.380
But if the final architecture of a brain is mathematically unpredictable from its starting code, how did early neurobiologists even begin to tackle this?

10:13.840 --> 10:23.280
I mean, looking at the sheer density of the human brain, 86 billion neurons tangled in a web of trillions of synapses, it must have felt like looking into an abyss.

10:23.340 --> 10:24.280
Oh, it was an abyss.

10:24.600 --> 10:29.400
And historically, early researchers were totally paralyzed by this exact information problem.

10:29.460 --> 10:29.840
I imagine.

10:29.840 --> 10:38.680
If we rewind a century to the 1920s and 30s, neurobiologists were using these really crude microscopes to look at slices of brain tissue stained with chemicals.

10:38.820 --> 10:39.460
And what did they see?

10:39.580 --> 10:45.060
They saw a forest of neurons so dense and chaotic, it completely defied description.

10:45.680 --> 10:59.080
They ran the math and quickly realized there is absolutely no way the human genome contains enough data storage to specify the exact coordinate location of every single one of these trillions of synaptic connections.

10:59.080 --> 11:01.580
Right, because the math simply doesn't allow for it.

11:02.000 --> 11:10.720
The genome is large, sure, but it's not large enough to hold a specific address and wiring diagram for 86 billion neurons.

11:11.240 --> 11:11.760
Precisely.

11:11.760 --> 11:20.260
So, faced with a genome too small to hold a blueprint, early neurobiologists concluded that the brain must start out as essentially a blank slate.

11:20.400 --> 11:20.920
Blank slate?

11:20.920 --> 11:21.240
Yeah.

11:21.660 --> 11:27.060
They theorized that a newborn's brain was wired somewhat randomly, like a chaotic web with no innate structure.

11:27.460 --> 11:34.560
In their view, the brain only became organized, structured, and smart through the rigorous process of experiencing the world and learning from the environment.

11:34.880 --> 11:41.640
Well, I am going to push back on those early biologists for a second because intuitively, the blank slate theory makes perfect sense to me.

11:41.760 --> 11:42.680
It does feel intuitive.

11:42.680 --> 11:48.640
Yeah. I mean, we know that if a child isn't exposed to language early on, they really struggle to learn it.

11:49.060 --> 11:51.720
We know trauma changes the physical structure of the brain.

11:52.120 --> 11:54.360
The environment clearly shapes us.

11:54.600 --> 11:55.020
That's true.

11:55.260 --> 12:02.180
And frankly, this historical biological theory is the exact foundation upon which modern artificial neural networks are built today.

12:02.600 --> 12:06.540
It is the absolute defining paradigm of modern machine learning.

12:06.540 --> 12:13.520
When computer scientists build an AI, they create a network of artificial nodes with completely random initial connections.

12:14.140 --> 12:16.760
The network is born completely ignorant.

12:17.160 --> 12:18.300
A literal blank slate.

12:18.500 --> 12:18.980
Exactly.

12:19.620 --> 12:22.180
They then feed it massive oceans of data.

12:22.360 --> 12:23.080
They train it.

12:23.680 --> 12:29.340
And through mathematically adjusting those connections based on the data, the network learns to recognize patterns.

12:30.060 --> 12:36.140
It starts as a random blank slate and brute forces its way to intelligence through environmental input.

12:36.140 --> 12:44.700
But the source material throws a massive wrench into this paradigm because biology eventually proved the absolute blank slate theory completely wrong.

12:44.880 --> 12:45.280
It did.

12:45.400 --> 12:49.540
And the piece of evidence Heisinger uses to dismantle it is the monarch butterfly.

12:49.920 --> 12:53.120
The monarch is biology's ultimate defiance of the blank slate.

12:53.740 --> 13:02.160
It proves definitively that wildly complex, precise behavioral information can exist in a brain prior to a single moment of learning.

13:02.160 --> 13:03.180
So picture this.

13:03.720 --> 13:05.060
It's early fall in North America.

13:05.780 --> 13:14.860
As the temperature drops, millions of monarch butterflies spread out across millions of square miles in the United States and Canada take to the sky.

13:15.020 --> 13:15.900
Millions of them.

13:16.080 --> 13:16.300
Yeah.

13:16.440 --> 13:19.720
And they begin a migration south that can span up to 3,000 miles.

13:20.400 --> 13:21.380
3,000 miles.

13:21.520 --> 13:26.520
They navigate thermal drafts, dodge predators, cross international borders.

13:26.640 --> 13:27.740
It's an incredible journey.

13:27.740 --> 13:29.860
But they don't just generally head south to Mexico.

13:30.580 --> 13:36.220
Millions of these insects converge on a few hyper-specific overwintering sites in the mountains of central Mexico.

13:36.800 --> 13:40.020
These sites cover a combined area of less than a single square mile.

13:40.240 --> 13:41.220
It's just wild.

13:41.280 --> 13:47.620
You have millions of tiny insects hitting a geographic target that is a microscopic speck on a continental map.

13:47.840 --> 13:50.360
It is an unparalleled feat of navigation.

13:50.540 --> 13:53.160
And for decades, scientists chased the mechanics of it.

13:53.380 --> 13:54.840
Are they using the sun as a compass?

13:54.840 --> 13:57.940
Are they detecting variations in the Earth's magnetic field?

13:58.060 --> 13:58.220
Okay.

13:58.520 --> 14:02.440
But the mechanics of navigation pale in comparison to the true mystery.

14:02.740 --> 14:03.260
Which is?

14:03.320 --> 14:04.720
The identity of the navigator.

14:04.780 --> 14:05.460
Oh, man.

14:05.840 --> 14:09.540
This is the detail that completely shatters the blank slate theory for me.

14:09.540 --> 14:19.640
The butterfly that lifts off from a milkweed plant in Ontario and successfully navigates 3,000 miles to a specific square mile of Mexican forest.

14:19.780 --> 14:20.040
Yeah.

14:20.220 --> 14:21.220
Has never been there before.

14:21.320 --> 14:21.580
Never.

14:21.840 --> 14:25.340
The full migration cycle takes three to five generations to complete.

14:26.200 --> 14:33.040
The butterflies over winter in Mexico fly a few hundred miles north into Texas in the spring, lay their eggs, and die.

14:33.340 --> 14:34.920
Then the second generation takes over.

14:35.020 --> 14:35.180
Right.

14:35.260 --> 14:37.460
They fly up to the Midwest, lay eggs, and die.

14:37.460 --> 14:39.540
A third generation makes it to Canada.

14:39.860 --> 14:48.440
By the time autumn arrives, the butterfly preparing to fly the full 3,000 miles south is the great-great-grandchild of the insect that made the journey the previous year.

14:48.520 --> 14:52.900
Which forces us to ask a question that AI researchers currently cannot answer.

14:53.340 --> 14:56.620
Where is the geographic and behavioral information coming from?

14:56.980 --> 14:59.500
It fundamentally cannot be learned from the environment.

15:00.240 --> 15:02.460
There is no elder butterfly guiding the swarm.

15:02.920 --> 15:04.540
The offspring has never seen the route.

15:04.540 --> 15:07.420
It has no memory of the mountains of Michoacan.

15:07.720 --> 15:14.480
And as we just rigorously established with our apple seed analogy, the route is not in the genes as a readable GPS map.

15:14.740 --> 15:15.000
Right.

15:15.100 --> 15:18.640
There are no latitudinal and longitudinal coordinates written in the DNA.

15:19.180 --> 15:26.420
So how does a biological neural network like the tiny clump of ganglia in a butterfly's head achieve this?

15:26.980 --> 15:32.040
How is an innate, flawless behavioral program physically encoded without learning?

15:32.040 --> 15:37.040
Well, contrast this biological miracle with how an artificial neural network handles navigation.

15:37.860 --> 15:38.580
Okay, how do they do it?

15:38.780 --> 15:43.900
To teach an AI to navigate a complex maze, you have to run it through millions of simulated failures.

15:44.320 --> 15:49.500
It relies entirely on random starting states combined with massive data sets of trial and error.

15:49.640 --> 15:51.100
It needs exhaustive training data.

15:51.100 --> 15:51.740
Exactly.

15:52.160 --> 16:02.560
But the monarch butterfly executes a perfect 3,000-mile flight on its very first attempt with zero training data from its own life experience.

16:03.380 --> 16:12.300
This proves that biology has a mechanism for incurring highly structured, evolved pre-wiring into a network before it ever interacts with the world.

16:12.660 --> 16:15.500
So we are staring at a massive divergence in philosophy.

16:16.320 --> 16:20.700
Computer science says build an empty network and bombard it with data until it learns.

16:20.700 --> 16:21.060
Yep.

16:21.640 --> 16:29.220
Biology says run a developmental algorithm that grows a network pre-installed with the physical architecture required to migrate across a continent.

16:29.680 --> 16:33.420
And to understand how a butterfly can inherently possess a root without a map,

16:33.800 --> 16:39.260
we have to look closely at the physical mechanics of how a brain wires itself together in the dark of a chrysalis or a womb.

16:39.560 --> 16:45.040
And to comprehend that physical wiring, we have to permanently discard the concept of the architectural blueprint.

16:45.700 --> 16:50.000
We must stop thinking of genes as a static schematic of chapters and wiring diagrams.

16:50.000 --> 16:51.060
We really do.

16:51.280 --> 16:54.980
Because if it's not a blueprint, what are we looking at under the microscope?

16:55.880 --> 17:01.640
Our sources provide an analogy for brain development that replaces the blueprint with something much more dynamic.

17:01.900 --> 17:04.720
I think this is one of the most helpful analogies in the deep dive.

17:04.840 --> 17:05.280
Me too.

17:05.280 --> 17:11.860
When a single neuron is trying to figure out where to send its axon, where to grow and connect,

17:12.480 --> 17:16.820
it is not behaving like a driver navigating a city with a GPS app.

17:16.960 --> 17:17.380
Right.

17:17.560 --> 17:24.940
Because a GPS navigation system relies on a fundamental assumption that the city's roads are already built,

17:25.160 --> 17:27.700
that they are static and waiting for you to drive on them.

17:27.700 --> 17:30.100
But the developing brain isn't a finished city.

17:30.740 --> 17:37.760
Imagine instead that you are trying to drive across a sprawling metropolis that is actively under construction while you are driving.

17:37.800 --> 17:39.260
A total nightmare of a commute.

17:39.500 --> 17:39.760
Right.

17:39.760 --> 17:47.600
You are moving down a street, and as your car rolls forward, construction crews are rapidly paving new roads mere inches in front of your bumper.

17:48.360 --> 17:52.680
Simultaneously, demolition crews are destroying the roads in your rearview mirror.

17:53.220 --> 17:55.000
The traffic lights aren't on timers.

17:55.400 --> 18:00.680
They are actively changing colors based on the chemical exhaust of the cars currently in the intersection.

18:00.960 --> 18:03.960
So the map itself is a living, morphing entity.

18:03.960 --> 18:13.700
Exactly. You only manage to arrive at your final destination because the geography of the city changes in direct interaction with your own movement through it.

18:13.980 --> 18:22.380
To translate your city analogy into cellular biology, that chaotic construction process is the absolute essence of self-assembly.

18:22.540 --> 18:23.260
Self-assembly.

18:23.440 --> 18:30.340
Yeah. When a brain is growing, it operates as a continuous dynamic feedback loop operating in both space and time.

18:30.660 --> 18:32.260
How does that work at the cellular level?

18:32.260 --> 18:39.920
Well, at the very first step of growth, specific environmental triggers cause certain parts of the genome to activate, producing specific proteins.

18:40.620 --> 18:45.860
These proteins physically alter the state of the cell and alter the chemical gradient of the tissue around it.

18:45.980 --> 18:47.300
Okay, so they change the environment.

18:47.380 --> 18:49.180
Right. And here is the magic trick.

18:49.620 --> 18:55.180
Those exact environmental alterations are the specific triggers required to turn on the next sequence of genes.

18:55.620 --> 18:58.980
Ooh. It is an algorithm manifesting physically.

18:58.980 --> 19:03.360
Step one physically creates the required conditions for step two.

19:04.040 --> 19:06.940
Step two physically creates the required conditions for step three.

19:07.020 --> 19:10.340
Exactly. And the most critical dimension here is time.

19:10.980 --> 19:14.140
Development is fiercely tied to temporal windows.

19:14.600 --> 19:14.940
What do you mean?

19:14.940 --> 19:26.900
A specific physical connection between two neurons might only be possible during a microscopic fleeting window of time when the local chemical environment is perfectly calibrated by the preceding steps.

19:26.980 --> 19:27.460
Oh, wow.

19:27.460 --> 19:37.460
If a neuron arrives at an intersection a millisecond too late, the chemical signal has dissipated, the road has been demolished, and that specific connection will never form.

19:37.780 --> 19:40.460
This highlights the glaring blind spot in the AI shortcut.

19:41.140 --> 19:41.900
It really does.

19:41.960 --> 19:50.160
Because modern AI developers look at this chaotic, time-dependent chemical soup of self-assembly and say, that's too messy. We will just skip it.

19:50.220 --> 19:51.440
Yeah, they want a clean system.

19:51.440 --> 20:01.980
They essentially use code to instantly conjure the finished city into existence. A massive grid of artificial neurons and synapses turn the power on and start pumping data through the streets.

20:02.360 --> 20:08.240
Because the assumption in Silicon Valley is that the developmental phase of a brain is merely the manufacturing process of the hardware.

20:08.500 --> 20:08.720
Right.

20:09.200 --> 20:19.300
They assume that as long as you can replicate the final hardware structure, you can bypass the messy growth phase and just mathematically tweak the connections to achieve intelligence.

20:19.300 --> 20:28.460
But Heisinger's text argues something completely different. In biology, the process of building the hardware is fundamentally where the innate intelligence is encoded.

20:28.840 --> 20:30.540
The self-assembly is the programming.

20:30.860 --> 20:47.640
The self-assembly is the programming. By skipping the algorithmic growth phase, AI isn't just saving time, it is potentially stripping out the fundamental architecture that allows a biological brain to possess innate general intelligence before learning even starts.

20:47.640 --> 20:59.000
So if we are dealing with a biological system that grows via a self-executing algorithm rather than being constructed from a blueprint, how do we even begin to quantify that mathematically?

20:59.280 --> 21:00.640
That's the million-dollar question.

21:00.720 --> 21:04.980
How does a computer scientist analyze an algorithm that physically builds a brain?

21:05.320 --> 21:15.340
This is where we cross into a deeply elegant concept from mathematics and theoretical computer science known as algorithmic information and the study of cellular automata.

21:15.340 --> 21:17.640
This is fascinating stuff.

21:17.720 --> 21:28.240
I will be honest, though. When the source text first veered into theoretical computer science, I tensed up. The math behind neural architecture can be incredibly dense.

21:28.460 --> 21:31.060
Oh, absolutely. It can be like reading a foreign language.

21:31.280 --> 21:39.520
Right. But Heisinger uses a specific mathematical game called Rule 110 to explain this, and it completely demystified the concept for me.

21:39.520 --> 21:50.100
Rule 110 is a foundational pillar in computer science because it visually demonstrates how laughably simple rules can generate infinite, unpredictable complexity.

21:50.620 --> 21:56.660
Let's walk through how this works because the mechanics are vital for the listener to grasp. Imagine a grid like a piece of graph paper.

21:56.700 --> 21:57.420
Okay. Got it.

21:57.420 --> 22:06.100
In the very top row of this grid, every single box is empty. Let's call an empty box an O, except for one single box right in the middle, which contains an X.

22:06.420 --> 22:10.740
So your starting state is just an infinite row of O's, a single X, and more O's.

22:11.120 --> 22:17.240
Exactly. As simple as it gets. Now, your goal is to generate the second row directly beneath the first.

22:17.240 --> 22:24.120
To do this, you follow one rigid, simple rule. You look at the boxes in the top row in groups of three, a triplet.

22:24.560 --> 22:27.840
So you look at a box, and it's immediate left and right neighbors.

22:28.000 --> 22:35.680
Yes. And based purely on what that triplet looks like, the rule dictates what goes in the box directly below the center of the triplet.

22:35.920 --> 22:36.740
Give us an example.

22:36.980 --> 22:43.200
So, for example, if the triplet you are looking at is OXO, the rule dictates you draw an X in the row below.

22:43.680 --> 22:46.440
If the triplet is OO, the rule tells you to draw an O.

22:46.440 --> 22:53.920
Okay. So you simply slide your focus across the top row, evaluating triplets, and drawing the corresponding X or O in the row beneath it.

22:54.020 --> 22:57.240
You execute the rule across the whole row, and now you have row two.

22:57.720 --> 23:03.360
Then you treat row two the new input, apply the exact same triplet rule, and generate row three.

23:03.740 --> 23:08.700
You repeat this iteratively. The output of one moment becomes the environmental input for the next.

23:08.700 --> 23:21.400
Intuition would tell us that such a rigid, simplistic triplet rule applied to a grid would just generate a boring, repetitive wallpaper pattern, like a solid block of X's or a perfectly symmetrical checkerboard.

23:21.520 --> 23:22.740
But it absolutely doesn't.

23:22.740 --> 23:29.180
As you run rule 110 down the page, the pattern that emerges looks like structured chaos.

23:29.180 --> 23:41.940
It generates jagged triangles that grow and collapse, long asymmetrical lines, and complex geometric clusters that look like little spaceships gliding across the grid, crashing into other structures and annihilating them.

23:42.000 --> 23:43.680
It is wildly unpredictable.

23:43.680 --> 23:49.640
It is. And mathematicians spent years studying this specific rule until they proved something monumental.

23:50.340 --> 23:53.000
Rule 110 is mathematically Turing-complete.

23:53.340 --> 23:57.880
Turing-completeness is a heavy conceptual term, but its implication is profound.

23:58.260 --> 23:59.580
What does it mean in this context?

23:59.980 --> 24:08.660
It means that the seemingly trivial grid of X's and O's, driven by a simple triplet rule, is theoretically capable of computing anything that can be computed in the universe.

24:08.820 --> 24:09.760
We really, anything.

24:09.760 --> 24:25.080
Yes, it is a universal computer. If you set up the initial row of X's and O's correctly, the cascading patterns crashing into each other could theoretically calculate the orbit of Jupiter, run a word processing program, or simulate a digital clock.

24:25.400 --> 24:34.580
The disparity between the input and the output is what's so jarring for me. The amount of information you need to write down the rule is minuscule. You could literally write it on a napkin.

24:35.120 --> 24:38.720
But the endpoint pattern it generates is infinitely complex.

24:38.720 --> 24:50.360
And this disparity perfectly encapsulates the difference between algorithmic information and endpoint information, which is vital for understanding why AI researchers and neurobiologists talk past each other.

24:50.440 --> 24:53.700
Okay, let's break that down. Algorithmic versus endpoint information.

24:53.920 --> 24:56.080
Let me use an analogy from digital data compression.

24:56.520 --> 25:06.460
Suppose I give you a massive 4K digital photograph of a perfectly clear, cloudless, solid blue sky, and I ask you to compress the file size. How does a computer do it?

25:06.460 --> 25:17.880
Oh, that's extremely easy. The compression algorithm just writes a tiny line of code that says paint the next 10 million pixels this exact hex code of blue. The resulting file size is, like, a few kilobytes.

25:17.880 --> 25:29.820
Precisely. That solid blue sky has very low algorithmic information. A tiny instruction creates the whole picture. But what if I hand you a 4K image of pure television static?

25:30.000 --> 25:31.780
Like just random white noise?

25:31.940 --> 25:39.480
Yes. Every single pixel is a completely randomized, unpredictable shade of black, white, or gray.

25:39.480 --> 25:53.720
You can't compress it. There is no pattern to mathematically summarize. To store that image, the computer literally has to write a list describing every single pixel. Pixel 1 is dark gray, pixel 2 is light gray, pixel 3 is white. The file size is gigantic.

25:53.920 --> 26:02.820
Exactly. The static image is high in endpoint information. Because there is no underlying rule generating the static, you must exhaustively describe the final state.

26:02.900 --> 26:03.560
Ah, I see.

26:03.560 --> 26:25.920
Now map this onto rule 110. The instruction manual for rule 110 is like the blue sky. The algorithmic information is incredibly low. But the massive grid it eventually generates is like the static, the endpoint information is incredibly dense, complex, and uncompressible. You cannot know what row 10,000 will look like without physically running the complication through all 9,999 preceding rows.

26:25.920 --> 26:37.540
So, applying this back to the biology of the brain, the genome is the algorithmic information. DNA holds a very small, highly compressed set of rules, analogous to the triplet rules of rule 110.

26:37.800 --> 26:38.020
Right.

26:38.020 --> 26:55.320
DNA doesn't hold a massive gigabyte file describing the static endpoint of a fully wired brain. It just contains rules for how proteins and cells interact locally. But by running those simple rules through the dimension of time, fueled by metabolic energy, the system generates massive endpoint complexity.

26:55.920 --> 26:57.360
A sentient human brain.

26:57.480 --> 27:02.700
Well, let's complicate that a bit, because a human brain isn't just a perfect, sterile execution of rule 110.

27:02.960 --> 27:04.520
Right. Biology is messy.

27:04.520 --> 27:19.580
A cellular automaton operates on a rigid mathematical grid where the rules never change. Biology is violently messy. Biology takes the concept of algorithmic growth and introduces stumble noise, chemical randomness, and rules that actively mutate as the system grows.

27:19.700 --> 27:20.580
Mutating rules.

27:20.580 --> 27:28.420
Yeah. The biological rules of the game at iteration 10 might be radically different from the rules at iteration 10,000, because the environment itself has changed.

27:28.420 --> 27:40.760
It would be like playing rule 110 on a piece of paper. But halfway down the page, someone spills coffee on it, the grid warps, the O's suddenly start acting like Q's, and the triplet rule reverses itself. It's chaotic.

27:40.760 --> 27:47.840
It is deeply chaotic. But from an evolutionary standpoint, that chemical noise and chaos isn't a bug. It's a feature.

27:48.360 --> 27:49.240
Really? How so?

27:49.540 --> 28:05.820
The noise forces the biological algorithm to be incredibly robust. It forces the developing brain to constantly adapt to tiny micro failures during self-assembly, which ultimately creates a finished brain capable of handling the messy, unpredictable, real world.

28:05.820 --> 28:23.140
So if biological brains require this chaotic, noisy, time-dependent growth to function, what happens when an AI researcher, whose entire discipline is built on eliminating noise, enforcing rigid mathematical constraints, and optimizing static structures, tries to collaborate with a neurobiologist?

28:23.240 --> 28:26.620
They literally cannot comprehend each other's foundational assumptions.

28:26.820 --> 28:29.240
And Heisinger witnessed this cultural clash firsthand.

28:29.480 --> 28:33.380
Yes. The source text highlights a fascinating sociological observation here.

28:33.520 --> 28:34.460
Tell us about the conferences.

28:34.460 --> 28:38.660
Heisinger deliberately attended two back-to-back scientific conferences.

28:39.260 --> 28:46.620
The first was an artificial life conference, packed with computer scientists focused on bottom-up engineering, computer simulations, and AI.

28:47.040 --> 28:47.320
Okay.

28:47.560 --> 29:00.520
The very next week, he attended the Molecular Mechanisms of Neuronal Connectivity Conference, which was packed with developmental biologists working top-down, dissecting physical brains to map out biological reality.

29:00.520 --> 29:03.040
And his observation is incredibly telling.

29:03.040 --> 29:13.900
Between these two massive gatherings of brilliant minds, both ostensibly trying to understand the nature of complex networks and intelligence, there was absolutely zero overlap in attendance.

29:14.440 --> 29:16.160
Not a single person went to both.

29:16.240 --> 29:16.800
Not one.

29:17.200 --> 29:25.100
The biologists are busy reverse-engineering the wet mechanics of nature, and the AI researchers are busy trying to simulate intelligence from pure math.

29:25.100 --> 29:32.980
To illustrate the profound friction between these two camps, Heisinger constructs a fictional scientific workshop featuring four archetypal scientists.

29:33.100 --> 29:40.960
I found these characters so helpful because they perfectly articulate the fierce debates currently raging in academic journals in Silicon Valley boardrooms.

29:40.960 --> 29:41.860
They really do.

29:41.940 --> 29:42.840
They aren't just straw men.

29:43.220 --> 29:43.700
Right.

29:43.700 --> 29:51.500
So we are introduced to Minda, a developmental geneticist who lives in the microscopic world of molecular mechanisms and biological rules.

29:52.380 --> 29:57.100
We have Alfred, a neuroscientist who studies macro-level brain function and neural circuits.

29:57.380 --> 30:08.240
And representing the tech sector, we have Aki, a highly pragmatic robotics engineer who only cares about building functional, capable machines, regardless of how they mimic biology.

30:08.240 --> 30:15.720
And finally, there is Pramesh, the AI researcher, deep in the mathematical weeds of writing deep learning algorithms.

30:15.880 --> 30:16.940
Yeah, quite the cast.

30:17.040 --> 30:22.700
I have to play devil's advocate here because as I read this section, I found myself furiously agreeing with Aki, the engineer.

30:22.840 --> 30:23.900
Really? Why is that?

30:24.400 --> 30:28.980
Well, if I am Aki, I am looking at the sheer capability of large language models right now.

30:29.340 --> 30:36.780
I'm watching artificial neural networks, pass a bar exam, write flawless Python code, and generate photorealistic video.

30:36.780 --> 30:42.300
And they are doing all of this using architectures that explicitly ignore biological development.

30:42.640 --> 30:43.280
That's a fair point.

30:43.460 --> 30:46.200
Aki's argument makes perfect pragmatic sense.

30:46.920 --> 30:50.300
She says, look, human engineering has always surpassed biology by discarding it.

30:50.900 --> 30:55.940
We built cars that drive significantly faster than cheetahs without using artificial muscle fibers.

30:56.520 --> 31:01.280
We engineered airplanes that fly vastly further than any bird without using flapping feathers.

31:01.500 --> 31:03.920
The classic planes don't flap their wings argument.

31:04.160 --> 31:04.460
Right.

31:04.460 --> 31:16.280
Why should AI researchers painstakingly recreate the messy, incredibly slow biology of a brain when we can just engineer past it with group force computing power in silicon?

31:16.660 --> 31:18.500
It is a highly compelling argument.

31:18.880 --> 31:22.300
And it is essentially the dominant religion of Silicon Valley right now.

31:22.600 --> 31:25.860
The belief that scaling up compute power will solve all deficiencies.

31:26.200 --> 31:27.280
But Alfred disagrees.

31:27.280 --> 31:34.440
Vigorously, let's dismantle the planes don't flap their wings analogy because Alfred, our neurobiologist, tears it apart beautifully.

31:34.560 --> 31:35.540
Okay, how does he counter it?

31:35.660 --> 31:39.120
Yes, a Boeing 747 flies further and faster than a Falcon.

31:39.740 --> 31:50.980
But a 747 cannot dart through a dense forest canopy at top speed, miraculously land on a trembling windblown twig, and instantly launch back into the air to catch a mosquito in mid-flight.

31:50.980 --> 31:52.420
Wow, good point.

31:52.640 --> 31:58.900
A 747 requires two miles of perfectly flat paved runway and an army of mechanics to function.

31:59.260 --> 32:02.480
So the plane is powerful, but it is utterly inflexible.

32:02.660 --> 32:03.180
Precisely.

32:03.680 --> 32:13.360
Biology possesses a fluid flexibility, extreme energy efficiency, and a resilience to catastrophic damage that brute force engineering hasn't even begun to touch.

32:13.520 --> 32:13.760
Right.

32:13.760 --> 32:23.260
Alfred argues that if you want the robust, adaptable intelligence of a biological brain, you might actually need the messy biological mechanisms that engineered it.

32:23.260 --> 32:28.520
This tension naturally brings up a running joke in the AI community that Alfred wields as a weapon.

32:29.280 --> 32:33.100
The assertion that human-level intelligence is always, you know, just 10 years away.

32:33.280 --> 32:33.760
Ah, yes.

32:34.080 --> 32:36.680
It is the perpetual mirage of computer science.

32:36.780 --> 32:37.540
It really is.

32:37.680 --> 32:37.820
Yeah.

32:37.820 --> 32:44.780
In the late 1950s, the pioneers of AI confidently predicted they would solve artificial intelligence over a summer research project.

32:44.840 --> 32:45.600
A summer project.

32:45.800 --> 32:45.960
Yeah.

32:46.120 --> 32:49.880
In the 1980s, expert systems were supposed to usher in AGI within a decade.

32:50.460 --> 32:56.240
And today, with deep learning, tech CEOs are once again promising that human-level AGI is only a decade out.

32:56.560 --> 33:04.420
But the biologists look under the hood of current deep learning models and see fundamental, insurmountable roadblocks.

33:04.420 --> 33:14.400
Pramesh, the AI researcher in our story, talks about the core engine of modern AI, a mathematical concept called backpropagation.

33:14.660 --> 33:22.200
Let's dive into the mechanics of this, because if you want to understand why AI isn't truly biological, you have to understand backpropagation.

33:22.880 --> 33:30.120
Backpropagation, short for the backward propagation of errors, is a mathematically elegant method for error correction in a neural network.

33:30.300 --> 33:30.900
How does it work?

33:30.900 --> 33:34.740
Imagine you are training an AI to recognize images of cats.

33:35.080 --> 33:38.120
You feed an image of a cat into the input layer of the network.

33:38.380 --> 33:44.360
The signal travels forward through millions of artificial synapses, and the network spits out an output guess.

33:44.680 --> 33:47.800
Because it's untrained, it guesses randomly and says, dog.

33:48.000 --> 33:48.920
So it made an error.

33:49.140 --> 33:49.960
A massive error.

33:50.280 --> 33:55.120
Now, the system calculates the mathematical difference between the network's guess dog and the correct answer, cat.

33:55.280 --> 33:57.380
That difference is the cost or the error signal.

33:57.380 --> 34:04.240
Backpropagation takes that global error signal and mathematically shoves it backward through the entire network.

34:04.760 --> 34:12.780
It reverse-engineers the pathway, calculating exactly how much each individual artificial synapse contributed to the wrong answer.

34:13.440 --> 34:15.400
Wow. That's intense.

34:15.400 --> 34:25.240
It then slightly adjusts the mathematical weight of every single connection so that the next time it sees that image, the network is infinitesimally closer to guessing cat.

34:25.660 --> 34:38.960
So you essentially pause the entire system, calculate a global failure rate from a God's eye perspective, and instantly transmit a perfectly calibrated mathematical correction backward through millions of connections simultaneously.

34:38.960 --> 34:41.720
It is incredibly powerful for machine learning.

34:41.920 --> 34:44.040
It is the engine driving the AI revolution.

34:44.660 --> 34:48.680
It's why your phone can recognize your face and translate Mandarin in real time.

34:49.020 --> 34:51.960
But here is the critical, devastating point Alfred makes.

34:52.400 --> 34:56.940
Biological brains definitively, unequivocally do not use backpropagation.

34:57.000 --> 34:59.220
Because a biological neuron doesn't have a supervisor.

34:59.220 --> 35:07.640
A neuron buried deep in your visual cortex doesn't magically know if you successfully caught a baseball or if you got hit in the face with it.

35:07.960 --> 35:11.640
It only knows what the neuron immediately adjacent to it is doing.

35:12.080 --> 35:20.520
It physically cannot receive a mathematically perfect, globally calculated error update transmitted backward across the synapse.

35:20.960 --> 35:23.820
Biological synapses are largely unidirectional.

35:24.360 --> 35:28.840
Chemical neurotransmitters flow from the axon of one neuron to the dendrite of the next.

35:28.840 --> 35:38.900
There is no physical mechanism to instantaneously freeze the brain, calculate a global cost function, and propagate a reverse mathematical gradient through millions of wet cells.

35:39.420 --> 35:41.760
Biology relies on local learning rules.

35:42.240 --> 35:46.200
Neurons firing together and wiring together based on local chemical environments.

35:46.600 --> 35:53.660
So AI has essentially built a massive jet engine backpropagation, which is fast and powerful at specific tasks.

35:53.940 --> 35:56.060
But it's fundamentally not a bird's wing.

35:56.060 --> 36:00.620
It's an engineered shortcut that bypasses how intelligence actually evolves.

36:01.240 --> 36:08.740
And this fundamental argument over how a network learns naturally culminates in the ultimate, most heavily contested test of any intelligence system.

36:09.140 --> 36:10.400
The mechanics of memory.

36:10.920 --> 36:12.920
How is information physically stored?

36:12.920 --> 36:20.420
This debate over memory is where the computer scientist and the biologist realize they are speaking completely different languages.

36:21.180 --> 36:26.300
Aki, the robotics engineer, asks what she thinks is a painfully straightforward question.

36:26.440 --> 36:28.120
Oh, the PIN code question.

36:28.120 --> 36:34.920
Yeah. She says, if I have a four-digit PIN code for my bank account, I know exactly how many bits of data that requires.

36:35.240 --> 36:38.000
And I know exactly where it is stored on a silicon hard drive.

36:38.520 --> 36:42.020
How many biological bits do you need in a brain to store those four digits?

36:42.560 --> 36:43.440
And where is it?

36:43.620 --> 36:49.060
To an engineer who designs systems where information is explicitly mapped to physical hardware coordinates,

36:49.460 --> 36:51.060
the question is totally logical.

36:51.260 --> 36:52.060
Makes perfect sense.

36:52.060 --> 36:56.540
But to Alfred, the neurobiologist, it is a conceptually nonsensical question.

36:57.020 --> 37:03.760
He has to explain that the computer memory address metaphor is arguably the most destructive analogy ever applied to the biological brain.

37:04.060 --> 37:07.560
Because in a computer, you open a document, type a password, and save it.

37:08.660 --> 37:15.140
The computer writes that specific data to a highly specific microscopic physical address on a silicon chip.

37:15.380 --> 37:15.520
Yeah.

37:15.640 --> 37:19.080
If you want the password, the computer queries that exact address.

37:19.300 --> 37:19.520
Right.

37:19.520 --> 37:23.280
But the brain does not have a physical address for your PIN code.

37:23.880 --> 37:27.720
There isn't a single number seven neuron glowing in your hippocampus.

37:27.920 --> 37:35.540
There is no isolated specific physical location that houses the memory of your grandmother's face or the smell of rain.

37:35.800 --> 37:38.820
Think about the fragility of the computer memory architecture for a second.

37:38.940 --> 37:39.140
Okay.

37:39.360 --> 37:42.740
If you take a magnet and corrupt a specific sector of a hard drive,

37:43.160 --> 37:48.340
whatever file was stored exactly at that physical address is irrevocably destroyed.

37:48.340 --> 37:48.960
It's gone.

37:48.960 --> 37:51.440
But the biological brain is constantly degrading.

37:51.600 --> 37:56.500
You lose thousands of neurons every single day to natural aging, minor head traumas, and cellular death.

37:56.840 --> 38:03.900
Yet you do not wake up one morning having forgotten the second digit of your PIN code because the specific neuron holding it died in the night.

38:04.160 --> 38:06.560
Because the information is not in a localized drawer.

38:06.560 --> 38:12.600
To bridge this conceptual gap, Pramesh offers a much better analogy from physics.

38:12.600 --> 38:17.040
He says memory in biological networks functions more like a hologram.

38:17.040 --> 38:25.040
A hologram is a profoundly counterintuitive piece of technology because of how it uses interference patterns to store light.

38:25.040 --> 38:27.800
Can you explain how it works compared to a normal photo?

38:28.520 --> 38:28.660
Sure.

38:28.860 --> 38:36.940
If you have a standard Polaroid photograph of a face and you take scissors and cut off the top right corner, you lose the right eye and the ear.

38:37.400 --> 38:39.020
The information in that corner is gone.

38:39.260 --> 38:39.360
Right.

38:39.360 --> 38:41.500
But a holographic plate works differently.

38:41.740 --> 38:48.840
If you have a holographic plate containing an image of a face and you break off a corner of the glass, you don't lose a specific piece of the face.

38:49.100 --> 38:49.620
Wait, really?

38:49.620 --> 38:53.460
If you shine a laser through the broken shard, the entire face is still projected.

38:53.780 --> 38:55.980
That blows my mind every time I think about it.

38:56.420 --> 39:02.900
Every single fragment of the hologram contains the data for the entire image just at a slightly lower resolution.

39:03.020 --> 39:03.520
Exactly.

39:04.020 --> 39:11.700
As you break the hologram into smaller and smaller pieces, the projected image just gets blurrier and noisier, but the whole image remains.

39:12.060 --> 39:19.060
The information required to generate the picture is radically decentralized and distributed across the entire physical surface of the plate.

39:19.060 --> 39:22.040
This is exactly how biological memory functions.

39:22.360 --> 39:23.300
So it's not in one spot.

39:23.520 --> 39:23.720
Right.

39:24.140 --> 39:28.500
A memory is a distributed pattern of synaptic weights across a massive, redundant network.

39:28.860 --> 39:32.260
You can physically damage parts of the network and the memory doesn't disappear.

39:32.640 --> 39:36.220
The recall might just become a little less sharp or take a little longer to access.

39:36.680 --> 39:43.200
Evolution heavily favors this holographic method because of what engineers call failing gracefully.

39:43.200 --> 39:49.040
A computer suffers a minor hardware error and failing crashes, the blue screen of death.

39:49.600 --> 39:55.700
A brain suffers minor hardware damage every day and gracefully compensates because the information is distributed.

39:56.040 --> 40:02.340
But Alfred, unwilling to let the AI researchers off the hook, throws down the ultimate trump card of biological messiness.

40:02.460 --> 40:02.840
What's that?

40:03.320 --> 40:07.820
He says, fine, let's agree that both advanced AI and biological brains use distributed networks.

40:07.820 --> 40:11.100
But how does your AI handle neuromodulators?

40:11.320 --> 40:13.140
Oh, the chemical soup, the hormones.

40:13.500 --> 40:17.760
Biology is not just electrical signals zipping cleanly down insulated wires.

40:17.960 --> 40:19.680
It is fundamentally chemical.

40:20.420 --> 40:29.520
Imagine neuromodulators, hormones like dopamine, serotonin, or adrenaline, as physical fluid waves washing over the physical structure of the brain.

40:29.820 --> 40:30.120
Okay.

40:30.120 --> 40:34.820
When you are suddenly terrified, a wave of adrenaline washes through the brain tissue.

40:35.620 --> 40:47.340
That chemical wave simultaneously alters the electrical firing thresholds and the physical connection states of millions of synapses all at once, based entirely on their physical location within the chemical gradient.

40:47.540 --> 40:48.340
So it's like a flood.

40:48.460 --> 40:52.800
It instantly, globally reconfigures the operational state of the entire network.

40:53.360 --> 40:57.720
If I am an AI researcher coding in Python, that sounds like an absolute nightmare to replicate.

40:57.720 --> 40:59.360
It is computationally horrific.

40:59.980 --> 41:03.820
How does an artificial neural network handle a localized chemical wash?

41:04.080 --> 41:04.640
It doesn't.

41:04.740 --> 41:05.640
They just ignore it.

41:05.740 --> 41:06.480
Pretty much.

41:06.940 --> 41:14.140
To accurately simulate neuromodulation, an AI model would either have to manually intervene and tune millions of artificial weights one by one,

41:14.440 --> 41:22.120
or it would have to run a wildly complex fluid dynamic simulation of chemical diffusion on top of its already massive electrical simulation.

41:22.460 --> 41:23.280
Which is impossible.

41:23.280 --> 41:32.200
AI architectures deliberately skip these messy chemical and developmental realities because they are computationally exorbitant and brutally hard to engineer.

41:32.640 --> 41:35.040
But the ultimate question of our deep dive is this.

41:35.860 --> 41:40.500
What if those messy, chaotic biological steps aren't just background noise?

41:41.200 --> 41:43.460
What if they are the entire point?

41:43.700 --> 41:45.880
That is the core hypothesis of the book.

41:45.880 --> 41:53.400
Our sources highlight an incredible, almost unsettling detail about how growing neurons physically wire themselves.

41:53.880 --> 41:58.680
They use these microscopic, finger-like protrusions called phillipodia.

41:59.020 --> 42:00.460
Philipodia are fascinating.

42:01.120 --> 42:10.100
As an axon grows, these tiny structural fingers constantly reach out, physically probing and sniffing the chemical environment around them in a seemingly random fashion.

42:10.100 --> 42:12.160
And they act as entirely autonomous agents.

42:12.360 --> 42:14.960
They aren't executing a command from a central processing unit.

42:15.440 --> 42:18.500
The book compares their behavior to players in a soccer match.

42:18.560 --> 42:19.360
That's a great analogy.

42:19.620 --> 42:20.140
Think about it.

42:20.320 --> 42:30.460
If a soccer coach put an earpiece in every player's ear and dictated every single step, every pass, every micro-movement from the sidelines, the team would collapse.

42:30.860 --> 42:33.820
It would be entirely too rigid to react to the opposing team.

42:33.820 --> 42:47.220
A functioning, robust soccer game relies on individual players making messy, localized decisions based solely on the chaotic environment directly in front of them while adhering to a general set of rules.

42:47.680 --> 42:52.760
That localized, semi-random decision-making is what creates a fluid, adaptable game.

42:52.960 --> 42:55.180
And that is the ultimate metaphor for self-assembly.

42:55.600 --> 42:57.240
The phillipodia are the soccer players.

42:57.240 --> 43:04.200
They make hyper-local, chemically-driven decisions, which create synapses, which slowly wire the overarching brain.

43:04.320 --> 43:05.660
And AI misses all of this.

43:05.840 --> 43:06.280
Completely.

43:06.800 --> 43:10.360
AI development violently skips this messy algorithmic growth phase.

43:11.080 --> 43:21.460
AI builds a massive, empty stadium, bolts the players rigidly to the field in static positions, and hopes that blasting them with massive amounts of training data will somehow teach them the essence of the game.

43:21.460 --> 43:26.480
While biology actually grows the players, the ball, and the stadium simultaneously.

43:26.740 --> 43:31.780
And the complex rules of intelligence emerge organically from the messy growth itself.

43:31.980 --> 43:32.580
Exactly.

43:33.120 --> 43:42.700
So after traversing from alien supercomputers to monarch butterflies, and from cellular automata to holographic memory, what does this all mean for you, the listener?

43:43.260 --> 43:47.460
Trying to navigate a world increasingly dominated by the hype of artificial intelligence.

43:48.380 --> 43:49.260
It's a lot to take in.

43:49.260 --> 43:57.420
The core takeaway from our deep dive today is that we need to radically, fundamentally change how we conceptualize information and intelligence.

43:58.020 --> 44:01.920
The genes inside you do not contain a map or a blueprint of who you are.

44:02.260 --> 44:04.980
They contain an algorithm that must be actively run.

44:05.240 --> 44:06.540
It must be grown over time.

44:07.120 --> 44:11.060
And as we've seen, the AI industry is currently taking a massive, deliberate shortcut.

44:11.600 --> 44:18.380
They are bypassing the algorithmic growth phase entirely, opting to build sterile, empty networks and train them from a blank slate.

44:18.380 --> 44:23.380
Which, to be fair, has undeniably yielded incredibly powerful, useful tools.

44:23.380 --> 44:33.920
But if our ultimate goal as a species is to truly understand and replicate general intelligence, we have to honestly acknowledge what is being left out of the silicon.

44:34.600 --> 44:45.620
The time, the physical energy, and the beautifully chaotic self-assembly that encodes innate knowledge and robustness long before a network ever attempts to learn its first fact.

44:45.620 --> 44:47.240
It's a completely different paradigm.

44:47.660 --> 44:51.460
Which leads us to one final, deeply provocative thought to leave you with.

44:51.780 --> 44:54.360
This comes from the final passages of Heisiger's text.

44:54.700 --> 45:01.380
And it's something I want you to seriously ponder the next time you encounter a sci-fi pitch about the utopian future of technology.

45:01.660 --> 45:02.880
The mind-uploading fantasy.

45:03.000 --> 45:03.220
Yes.

45:03.220 --> 45:10.200
There's a massive subculture right now, transhumanism, that preaches the inevitability of eventually uploading our minds to a computer.

45:10.400 --> 45:10.780
Right.

45:10.980 --> 45:21.680
The dream of escaping our biological bodies, taking all of your memories, your distinct personality, your peon codes, your traumas, and perfectly transferring that data to a silicon server to live forever.

45:22.020 --> 45:24.320
It is the ultimate Silicon Valley fantasy.

45:24.960 --> 45:28.300
But interrogate that fantasy with everything we have discussed today.

45:28.300 --> 45:49.560
If human memory and human intelligence are fundamentally not stored in dedicated localized bits, if your mind is not a static file saved to a specific biological address, but rather your mind exists as a continuous active algorithmic state distributed across a constantly changing chemical network.

45:49.820 --> 45:51.660
What does that mean for mind uploading?

45:52.140 --> 45:54.960
It means the entire premise is mathematically flawed.

45:54.960 --> 46:00.920
You couldn't just copy and paste your brain's data onto a hard drive, like dragging a folder onto a USB stick.

46:01.200 --> 46:05.880
The data of you literally does not exist in a static form that can be copied.

46:06.160 --> 46:06.760
It just doesn't.

46:06.880 --> 46:10.540
To truly transfer a human mind, you couldn't just read the genetic and synaptic code.

46:10.740 --> 46:19.080
To capture the active, unfolding algorithmic information of your consciousness, you might literally have to figure out how to chemically grow your brain again in the digital world.

46:19.180 --> 46:20.780
You can't pause a fire and copy it.

46:20.800 --> 46:22.020
You have to recreate the combustion.

46:22.440 --> 46:22.800
Exactly.

46:22.800 --> 46:29.040
And until we understand how a brain physically self-assembles its own fire, uploading it remains an illusion.

46:29.660 --> 46:34.380
Keep that philosophical hurdle in mind the next time someone offers to permanently back up your soul to the cloud.

46:34.380 --> 46:42.180
We'll see you next time.
